              "end": 60,
              "start": 60
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Ms. Graeff argues that custody and voluntariness are mixed questions of fact and law; historical facts receive deference, but the ultimate question of custody and voluntariness is reviewed de novo."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Graeff",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "1648089627b37d6cc9dc179b480f2bbdb499570bb4ab3fb74223e1d4c84eb0a2",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-1648089627b3-0003-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 64,
              "start": 64
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Ms. Graeff contends that Detective Johns cured the impropriety by informing Blake that there would be no more questioning, leaving Blake free to choose whether to speak or remain silent."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Kenneth W. Ravenell",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "1648089627b37d6cc9dc179b480f2bbdb499570bb4ab3fb74223e1d4c84eb0a2",
            "evidence_ids": [
